Q: How to convert String Array to String in Swift A: <p>This should work.</p>
<pre><code>let string = [&quot;1&quot;,&quot;2&quot;,&quot;3&quot;,&quot;4&quot;,&quot;5&quot;]

let newString = &quot;\&quot;[\&quot;&quot; + string.joined(separator: &quot;\&quot;,\&quot;&quot;) + &quot;\&quot;]\&quot;&quot;

print(newString) // Prints &quot;[&quot;1&quot;,&quot;2&quot;,&quot;3&quot;,&quot;4&quot;,&quot;5&quot;]&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Edit: The best way is to use <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/52293802/7734643">@vadian</a>'s answer. That said, there may be a more suitable approach depending on your requirements.</p>

Q: Show message if all textfields are complete, correctly in swift 4? A: <p>You are not <strong>check</strong>ing if the <strong>second text field is empty</strong> or not anywhere. ( Consider renaming <code>filterTextField</code> )</p>
<pre><code>if isEmailAddressValid &amp;&amp; !filterTextField.text?.isEmpty {
    print(&quot;Email address is valid&quot;)
} else {
    print(&quot;Email address is not valid&quot;)
    displayAlertMessage(messageToDisplay: &quot;Email address is not valid&quot;)
    return
}
</code></pre>

Q: Is there a way to iterate over a Dictionary of the form Key:[Value1:[Value2]]? A: <p>You can do it like this.</p>
<pre><code>for (key, value) in col_read {
    print(key)
    for (innerKey, innerValue) in value {
        print(innerKey)
        for number in innerValue {
            print(number)
        }
    } 
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p><strong>P.S</strong> - Use <em>lowerCamelCase</em> for variable names as mentioned in the <a href="https://swift.org/documentation/api-design-guidelines/#naming" rel="nofollow noreferrer">API Design Guidelines</a>.</p>
